<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Catalyst.entitlements PLUS the Keychain group — used only when a
  Developer ID provisioning profile is embedded in the bundle.

  Read the long comment in Catalyst.entitlements first. The short version:
  keychain-access-groups is a RESTRICTED entitlement. macOS validates it
  against a profile inside the bundle, and a build with no profile is not
  merely denied the Keychain — it is SIGKILLed before main() with
  "Restricted entitlements not validated, bailing out". That is why this
  is a separate file rather than a couple of extra keys in the other one:
  the wrong one of the two produces an app that does not start, and the
  choice between them is made by scripts/build-catalyst.sh from whether a
  profile is actually there to authorise it.

  With this file in play the journal, the fasting log, the coordinates
  and the sync identity go back to the Keychain, and durableWrite.ts stops
  keeping its plaintext copy — see the fallback note there for what this
  is worth.

  The group is spelled literally. `$(AppIdentifierPrefix)` and
  `$(CFBundleIdentifier)` are build-time expansions and this file is fed
  straight to codesign, so nothing expands. It is the Catalyst bundle
  identifier, which carries the maccatalyst prefix that
  DERIVE_MACCATALYST_PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER adds — not the iOS one.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>GAW23HT439.group.com.prayerapp</string>
	</array>
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>GAW23HT439.maccatalyst.com.hassan.prayerapp</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
